   "l": "That whereby the great rivers and the sea can be king of the hundred valleys —\nit is because they are good at putting themselves below them,\nand so they can be king of the hundred valleys.\nTherefore: wishing to be above the people,\none must by speech put oneself below them;\nwishing to go ahead of the people,\none must by one's own person put oneself behind them.\nTherefore the sage takes his place above, and yet the people do not feel the weight;\nhe takes his place in front, and yet the people take no harm.\nTherefore all under heaven gladly push him up and are not sated with him;\nbecause he does not contend,\nnone under heaven can contend with him.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The great rivers and the sea can be king over the hundred valleys because they are good at lying below them; that is how they come to be their king. So one who would stand above the people must speak as their inferior; one who would go before them must put his own person last. The sage stands above them and they feel no weight; he stands in front of them and they take no harm. All under heaven are glad to push him up and never tire of him; and because he does not contend, no one under heaven can contend with him.",
   "n": "所以 … 者 is a frame. 所以 'that whereby' opens it, 者 closes it, and everything between is the reason: 'the-whereby-the-rivers-and-the-sea-can-be-king-of-the-hundred-valleys one'. English has to unpick it into 'the reason why'.\n\nThe chapter runs on position-words used as verbs, and that is the whole argument made out of grammar. 下 is 'below', but 下之 is 'to put oneself below them'; 上民 is 'to stand above the people'; 先民 'to go in front of the people'; 後之 'to put oneself behind them'. Four of them, none marked as a verb in any way. The low place is what makes the high place possible, and the sentences do it before they say it.\n\n厭 does not mean 'dislike' — that is its modern sense. It is to have had one's fill, to be sated to the point of turning away: the people never get their fill of him. And 不重 is not 'he is not heavy' but 'they do not feel the weight' — the weight of a ruler being taxes, levies and forced labour.\n\n與 has three readings and three senses. In 莫能與之爭 it is yǔ, the plain 'with'.\n\nThis is one of the chapters recovered on the Guodian bamboo slips, buried around 300 BC — the oldest witness to any part of the book, three centuries older than the received text — and it stands there in wording close to what you are reading, though not identical character for character.",
   "p": true
